---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import CssUnitConverter from '../../components/CssUnitConverter.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="CSS Unit Converter (PX / REM / EM)" description="Convert between px, rem, and em based on your root font size — avoid guessing at design handoff.">
  <CssUnitConverter client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why REM is usually preferred</h2>
  <p>REM scales consistently with the root font size regardless of nesting, making layouts more predictable when users change their browser's default font size for accessibility.</p>

  <h2>Common CSS Unit Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using EM for nested components</strong>, where sizes compound unexpectedly based on parent font sizes at each nesting level.</li>
    <li><strong>Hardcoding pixel values</strong> for font sizes, ignoring users who've changed their browser's default font size for accessibility reasons.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Convert CSS Units</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Converting a design mockup's pixel measurements to rem for implementation</li>
    <li>Understanding the actual computed size of an em value in a nested component</li>
    <li>Standardizing on rem-based sizing for a more accessible, scalable layout</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>CSS Unit Conversion FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's the default root font size?</strong> 16px is the near-universal browser default, which this tool uses unless you specify a different root size — check your project's actual CSS if it overrides the <code>html</code> element's font-size.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I use rem or px for media query breakpoints?</strong> Rem is generally preferred since it respects a user's browser zoom/font-size settings, while px-based breakpoints don't scale the same way for users with accessibility font adjustments.</p>
</ToolLayout>
